@@ PE1 | Agile
In a game studio, the creative director wants the game to be as much fun as possible, while the monetisation team pushes for more in-game purchases. Design discussions keep stalling. What should the producer do?
A) Facilitate agreement on a few design pillars and success measures that balance both views
B) Let the creative director decide every design question, since the game's quality depends on it
C) Let the monetisation team decide, since the studio needs revenue to stay in business at all
D) Ask each group to design its own version of the game and test both with players at the end
= A
? Shared design pillars and agreed success measures give everyone the same reference for decisions. Letting one side win or splitting the work does not create a common vision.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
The project manager drafted the charter for a residential development alone, and the key stakeholders feel no ownership of it. What should the project manager do?
A) Revisit the charter with the key stakeholders in a workshop
B) Ask the sponsor to sign the charter without further discussion
C) Send the charter to all stakeholders and ask for written approval
D) Leave the charter as it is, since it has already been written
= A
? Developing the charter collaboratively builds shared understanding of the objectives and commitment to them.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Developers in three studios in different countries identify with their own studio rather than with the game they are building together. What should the producer do?
A) Create shared goals and rituals around the game, such as joint demos and a common team charter
B) Let each studio keep its own goals, since local identity motivates the developers in each studio
C) Merge the three studios into a single location, so that everyone works in the same building
D) Rank the studios against each other each month, so that competition drives them to perform better
= A
? Shared goals, rituals and agreements build a common identity across locations. Ranking studios against each other would deepen the divisions.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Before development starts, the product owner asks the team to write the press release that will announce the finished product to customers. What is the purpose of this exercise?
A) To align the team on the customer outcome, working backwards from it
B) To prepare the marketing materials early and save time at launch
C) To check whether the team's writing skills are good enough
D) To fix the scope at the start so that it cannot change during development
= A
? "Working backwards" from a future press release makes the customer benefit concrete and gives the team a shared picture of success.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
New subcontractors join a large building project every few weeks, and many do not know the project's objectives or site rules. What should the project manager introduce?
A) A structured induction covering the project's objectives, values and safety rules
B) A copy of the full project management plan for each subcontractor to read on arrival
C) A rule that subcontractors may start work only after the main contractor's training course
D) A monthly meeting where the project manager presents the latest status to all subcontractors
= A
? A consistent induction gives every newcomer the same understanding of the project's goals, expectations and rules from their first day.

@@ PE1 | Agile | multi
Which two characteristics make a product vision effective? (Choose two.)
A) It describes the target customers and the value they will get
B) It is short and memorable enough to guide everyday decisions
C) It lists every feature that the product will eventually contain
D) It includes the detailed schedule and the budget for development
E) It is written by the legal department to avoid any commitments
= A, B
? An effective vision is customer-focused, inspiring and brief, so that people can use it to make decisions. Feature lists and schedules belong elsewhere.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
A competitor's drug, which works in a similar way, has just failed in clinical trials, and the biotech team starts to doubt its own project. What should the project manager do?
A) Review with the team how this project differs, using the data, and reaffirm the vision
B) Tell the team that the competitor's failure is irrelevant and that no discussion is needed
C) Ask the sponsor to announce bonuses, so that the team stays motivated despite the news
D) Stop the project immediately, since a similar drug has already failed in the clinic
= A
? A shared vision needs to survive new information. An honest, evidence-based discussion of the differences rebuilds confidence, or shows if the direction should change.

@@ PE2 | Agile
Before a game launch, the art lead wants the team to work overtime, while the engineering lead refuses on behalf of his developers. The disagreement is becoming heated. What should the producer do?
A) Facilitate using data and team norms, and take scope options to the product owner
B) Side with the art lead, since overtime before a launch is normal and expected in game studios
C) Side with the engineering lead, since developers should never be asked to work any overtime
D) Ask the studio director to impose a decision on both leads so that the conflict ends quickly
= A
? Using facts and agreed norms keeps the discussion on the problem. The real trade-off, scope versus date, is a product decision.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
The general contractor and the architect blame each other for a design error that requires rework. The dispute is starting to delay the work. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree the fix now and settle responsibility separately under the contract
B) Wait until the dispute has been settled before any rework is started on the building
C) Ask the general contractor to pay for the rework, since it built the faulty element
D) Ask the architect to pay for the rework, since the design contained the error
= A
? Separating the urgent technical fix from the commercial dispute keeps the project moving, while responsibility is resolved through the contract's mechanisms.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
At a public meeting about new traffic cameras, a citizens' group and the city's transport department start arguing loudly. What should the project manager do?
A) Calm the discussion, acknowledge the concerns and refocus on the shared goal of road safety
B) End the meeting immediately and continue the discussion with each group separately by email
C) Support the transport department, since it is the project's sponsor and approved the cameras
D) Let both sides argue until they run out of energy, since this will reduce tension in future
= A
? Facilitating respectfully, acknowledging concerns and focusing on common interests turns a confrontation into a constructive discussion.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Two scientists disagree about how to interpret the results of a key experiment, and the disagreement is delaying a decision. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree the analysis method together, and involve an independent statistician if needed
B) Accept the interpretation of the more senior scientist, since she has more experience
C) Repeat the experiment until both scientists reach the same conclusion on their own
D) Report both interpretations to the sponsor and ask the sponsor to choose between the two
= A
? Disagreements about data are best resolved by agreeing objective methods and, if necessary, independent expertise.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
In principled negotiation, what should the negotiators focus on?
A) Interests rather than positions
B) Winning as much as possible
C) Their opening positions
D) The other side's weaknesses
= A
? Principled negotiation separates the people from the problem, focuses on interests rather than positions, generates options for mutual gain and uses objective criteria.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
The electrical and plumbing subcontractors argue about who should work first in each apartment, and progress is slowing. What should the project manager do?
A) Bring both together to agree a sequenced work plan for each floor
B) Let the subcontractor that arrives on site first each day choose
C) Ask both subcontractors to work in the apartments at the same time
D) Replace one of the subcontractors with a firm that is more flexible
= A
? Coordinating the sequence of work with both parties removes the cause of the conflict and improves flow.

@@ PE2 | Agile
A junior designer tells the scrum master that the lead designer always dismisses her ideas in team discussions. What should the scrum master do?
A) Coach the lead designer and use inclusive techniques, such as silent brainstorming
B) Tell the junior designer to speak more loudly and confidently in future design discussions
C) Ask the lead designer to stop attending the team's design discussions for the next few sprints
D) Report the lead designer's behaviour to the studio's human resources department immediately
= A
? Coaching and inclusive facilitation techniques give everyone a voice and address the behaviour constructively.

@@ PE2 | Predictive | multi
Which two conflict resolution approaches usually provide only temporary relief rather than resolving the underlying problem? (Choose two.)
A) Smoothing (accommodating)
B) Withdrawing (avoiding)
C) Collaborating (problem solving)
D) Consensus building
E) Confronting the problem
= A, B
? Smoothing plays down differences and withdrawing postpones the issue; neither resolves its cause. Collaborating addresses the root of the conflict.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A new project manager introduces strict rules on core working hours without consulting the self-organising team. Morale drops. What should the project manager have done?
A) Facilitated the team in defining its own working agreements
B) Introduced the rules gradually over several sprints instead
C) Asked human resources to announce the rules on his behalf
D) Applied the rules only to the newest members of the team
= A
? Self-organising teams own their working agreements. Leaders facilitate and set boundaries, but people commit more to agreements they help create.

@@ PE3 | Predictive | match
Match each leadership style with its description.
1) Transformational
2) Transactional
3) Servant
4) Laissez-faire
A) Inspires with a vision and encourages innovation
B) Uses rewards and corrective action based on performance
C) Puts the team's needs first and removes obstacles
D) Leaves decisions largely to the team, with little involvement
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Effective project leaders adapt their style to the situation and the team's needs.

@@ PE3 | Agile
Which recognition practice fits a self-organising team best?
A) Peer-to-peer recognition, for example thanking colleagues in retrospectives
B) An employee-of-the-month award chosen by the project manager alone after each sprint
C) Bonuses for the individuals who complete the most story points
D) Recognition only at the end of the project, in the final report
= A
? Peer recognition reinforces collaboration and is timely. Rewards for individual output can undermine teamwork.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
The project manager must decide quickly how to handle a vendor problem, but wants to benefit from the team's knowledge. Which decision-making style fits best?
A) Consultative: gather the team's input, then decide
B) Consensus: wait until everyone fully agrees
C) Autocratic: decide alone without asking anyone
D) Delegated: let the vendor decide on its own
= A
? A consultative style combines speed with the team's expertise: the leader seeks input but remains responsible for the decision.

@@ PE3 | Agile
Team members complain that one colleague rarely contributes to shared work. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Help the team raise it directly with the colleague, referring to their working agreements
B) Report the colleague to the functional manager so that formal action can be taken
C) Reassign the colleague's tasks to others, so that the team's progress is protected
D) Ignore the complaints, since self-organising teams must solve such problems alone without help
= A
? Self-organising teams address performance issues through direct, respectful feedback based on their own agreements. Escalation follows only if this does not work.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
After a merger, workers worried about keeping their jobs show little interest in the project's recognition scheme. Which theory best explains this?
A) Maslow's hierarchy of needs
B) McGregor's Theory X and Theory Y
C) Vroom's expectancy theory
D) Tuckman's ladder of development
= A
? According to Maslow, unmet lower-level needs, such as security, take priority over higher-level needs, such as esteem and recognition.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
The project manager wants a capable team lead to take ownership of the façade work package. How should the work be delegated?
A) Define the outcome, the authority, the resources and the check-in points
B) Hand over the work and avoid any contact until it has been completed
C) Delegate the tasks but keep all of the decisions with the project manager
D) Ask the team lead to report on every task every day for the first month
= A
? Effective delegation makes the expected outcome, the authority and the support clear, with agreed check-ins rather than micromanagement.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
A project manager coaches a team member by exploring her goal, the current reality, the options available and the way forward. Which coaching model is this?
A) GROW
B) ADKAR
C) SMART
D) DMAIC
= A
? The GROW model (goal, reality, options, will or way forward) structures coaching conversations so that the person finds their own solution.

@@ PE3 | Agile | multi
Which two practices help prevent burnout in a game studio approaching launch? (Choose two.)
A) Planning work based on sustainable capacity
B) Making overtime visible and limiting it
C) Rewarding the people who work longest
D) Hiding overtime from senior management
E) Cancelling rest breaks until launch day
= A, B
? Sustainable pace depends on realistic planning and on treating overtime as an exception that is visible and limited.

@@ PE4 | Agile
How can a game studio engage its player community during development?
A) Through community channels and early-access builds, with feedback feeding the backlog
B) By keeping the game secret until launch, so that competitors cannot copy its features
C) By asking only the studio's own staff to play the game and report their impressions
D) By reading the reviews after launch and fixing the issues that players mention most
= A
? Early and continuous engagement with players provides feedback when it is still cheap to act on it.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
Tenants of an existing building must be relocated while it is redeveloped. How should the project engage them?
A) Early, with clear information, a contact person and practical relocation support
B) Shortly before demolition, so that the tenants have less time to object to the plan
C) Through the building's owner only, since the tenants have no contract with the project
D) Through a legal notice, since the tenants' leases allow the owner to redevelop
= A
? People strongly affected by a project need early, clear and supportive engagement. Late or purely legal engagement creates resistance and harm.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
Privacy advocates publicly criticise the smart city sensors, claiming that they will track citizens. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage them early, share the privacy design and invite their review
B) Ignore them, since the sensors comply with the data protection law
C) Ask the city's lawyers to send them a formal warning about their claims
D) Remove all sensors from the plan to avoid any further criticism
= A
? Engaging critics transparently can improve the design and build trust. Ignoring or threatening them increases opposition.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A biotech company wants patients' perspectives on the design of a clinical trial. How should it engage them?
A) Through patient advocacy groups, for example in an advisory board
B) Through the trial's marketing materials once recruitment has started
C) Through the regulator, which represents patients' interests in trials
D) Through the doctors only, since patients cannot judge trial designs
= A
